TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Preliminary Report (Part 1)","overview":"Plan a simple space mission that benefits Australia and present preliminary design work to guide the full mission assignment. Explain the mission concept and rationale, plan how the assignment will be completed, identify information sources and software, and show evidence of technical progress."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Mission concept and rationale — What will it do, who are the users, why is it needed?","weight":40,"max":40,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"A clear and complete description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Clearly relates required mission function to users and their needs. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Sufficient description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Identification of users and their needs. Descriptions feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"A poor description with mission goals, objectives and requirements unclear. Insufficient identification of users and their needs."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Project plan — How you plan to complete the assignment, including either a Gantt chart or a timetable and information sources you plan to use","weight":30,"max":30,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The plan for project completion is robust and has provision for project variations and contingencies. The plan fits within the narrative set out by the mission concept – the student makes clear why the plan is developed this way in the context of the mission. Appropriate references to information sources are included."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Plan is presented but lacks some detail. However, the plan is enough to believe that the project is feasible."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Plan is not included or does not have sufficient detail to demonstrate that the project can be completed."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Preliminary progress — Technical content and evidence of sufficient work done to date","weight":10,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Good preliminary set of results included with brief description and/or interpretation. Clearly on track for completion."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Minor work completed on the project. Results description or interpretation may be insufficient."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful work completed."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":20,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical structure. The report is very easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Mission Concept & Rationale"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan / Image 1"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan (Table 2)"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Preliminary Progress"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Preliminary Progress (Table 3)","issue":"Sun-synchronous inclination of 97.8° is stated without the J2 nodal-precession working, so the key orbit assumption cannot be checked."},{"where":"Preliminary Progress","issue":"Coverage conclusion is qualitative; no explicit swath-count or revisit calculation (e.g. passes needed vs daylight passes per week) is shown to prove weekly coverage fails."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"Requirement values (10 m GSD, weekly revisit, 24 h delivery) are asserted rather than derived from the cited sources or sensor trade."},{"where":"References","issue":"Reference [6] is a NOAA climate-change page but is cited as the source of BoM sea-surface-temperature products; no BoM source is listed."},{"where":"Project Plan (Image 1)","issue":"Gantt shows STK Simulations running through Week 10, contradicting the text that final design and simulation finish by end of Week 9."}],"overall":"Strong, well-structured preliminary report: a well-argued Australian reef-monitoring mission with clear users, a justified STK-based plan with contingencies and sources, and correct preliminary orbit calculations that honestly expose a coverage shortfall. Main gaps are unshown inclination working, unquantified revisit analysis, and one mismatched citation.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":36.0,"max":40.0,"reasoning":"Clear goal, four numbered objectives/requirements, named users with distinct needs, and rationale grounded in bleaching evidence and cited literature. Objectives are traced back to user needs. Slight weakness: requirement values (10 m, weekly, 24 h) are asserted rather than derived from sources.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"Give reef managers direct, repeatable observations of coral colour over the whole Great Barrier Reef, so that bleaching is detected within days"},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"The satellite would be operated by the Australian Institute of Marine Science (AIMS). Its customers are the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park Authority"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":27.0,"max":30.0,"reasoning":"Gantt chart with dated weeks, per-task descriptions, sequencing justified by mission logic, a risk/contingency table with triggers and responses, and a source table. Software choice (STK) justified against mission questions. Minor: STK Simulations bar runs into Week 10 despite text saying completion by Week 9.","evidence":[{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"The order follows the mission: the imaging requirements (objectives 1 and 2) fix the orbit altitude and local time"},{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"Week 10 is float as well as editing time: no new design work is planned in it."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Hand calculations for a, T, orbits/day, SSO inclination, ground-track shift and swath are numerically correct and interpreted, exposing a genuine coverage shortfall that feeds the contingency plan. Inclination is stated without working, and the coverage conclusion is qualitative rather than quantified.","evidence":[{"where":"Preliminary Progress","quote":"the reef is up to about 250 km wide, so a 60 km swath needs at least five neighbouring passes"},{"where":"Preliminary Progress","quote":"A first STK scenario with this orbit has been set up, and its period agrees with the hand calculation."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Logical structure, clear headings, readable prose, labelled figure and tables, consistent formatting, and in-text citations matching the reference list. Deductions: reference [6] (NOAA climate-change page) is cited for BoM sea-surface-temperature products, and [4] is a news article rather than the primary method source.","evidence":[{"where":"Image 1","quote":""},{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"Figure 1: Project Gantt chart, Weeks 2 to 10 (gold = completed, blue = planned, red lines = report deadlines)."}]}]}
